## Vinaya Piṭaka
## Vinayasaṅgaha-aṭṭhakathā
## Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sammāsambuddhassa
## Introduction to the Text
Having paid homage to the Triple Gem, the refuge of all beings, I shall present, in a single volume and without confusion, the decisions not found in the Pāḷi [canonical texts] but scattered across many treatises, for the sake of the proficiency in Vinaya of those bhikkhus who practice meditation.

## The table of contents (mātikā) for these decisions is as follows:
"Sleeping during the day, requisites, and the administration of medicine;
Paritta, hospitality, requesting, and supporting families.

"Kinds of fish and meat, things that should not be touched, making formal assignments (adhiṭṭhāna) and transfers (vikappanā);
Staying without the robes, and the storing of goods.

"Buying and selling, receiving gold and silver;
Diverting gains through giving and taking in trust.

"Digging the earth, destroying vegetation (bhūtagāma), two kinds of sleeping together;
Departing after spreading bedding in a communal (saṅghika) monastery.

"The four time-limited things (kālika), the four grounds for what is allowable (kappiyabhūmi);
Receiving food and so on, rejection and invitation (pavāraṇā).

"Ordination (pabbajjā), dependence (nissaya), the boundary (sīmā), performing the Uposatha and Pavāraṇā;
Entering the rains-retreat, duties, and the distribution of the four requisites.

"Kathina, heavy articles (garubhaṇḍa), decisions on accusations (codanā) and so on;
Rehabilitation from a heavy offense, formal acts (kamma) and non-acts, and miscellaneous [decisions]."

1. Decision on sleeping during the day. Among those terms in the table of contents, the meaning of the word "divāseyyā" should be understood as sleeping during the day. Regarding sleeping during the day, the decision is as follows: Because the Blessed One said, "Bhikkhus, I allow a bhikkhu who is resting during the day to rest after closing the door," a bhikkhu who sleeps during the day must sleep after closing the door. Although it is not explicitly stated in the Pāḷi text as "such and such an offense is committed," the Aṭṭhakathā states that a dukkhaṭa offense is incurred by a bhikkhu who sleeps without closing the door, because the Blessed One gave permission to sleep after closing the door when an incident occurred where a fault arose from sleeping with the door open.
